    <h1>Chinese Chilli Varieties: A Heat &amp; Region Guide</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Chinese chillies span almost the entire heat spectrum — from sweet Xinjiang pepper under 500 SHU
      to extra-hot Jixin over 70,000 SHU. The variety is the first decision in any specification.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Chinese chillies span nearly the whole usable heat range — from sweet Xinjiang pepper under 500 SHU to extra-hot Jixin over 70,000 SHU. Because heat, colour and flavour are set by variety and vary independently, the variety name is the first — and most important — decision in any specification.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The heat spectrum at a glance</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Heat band</th><th>SHU</th><th>Representative varieties</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Sweet</td><td>&lt;500</td><td>Xinjiang sweet pepper, Red Dragon 23</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Low heat</td><td>&lt;10,000</td><td>Yidu red, Wangdu</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Medium heat</td><td>10,000–20,000</td><td>Xian, Jinta, Erjingtiao</td></tr>
        <tr><td>High heat</td><td>20,000–50,000</td><td>Sanying, Zidantou, Seven-Star</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Extra hot</td><td>&gt;50,000</td><td>Jixin, Xiaomi, Chilli King</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The five growing regions</h2>
    <p>
      Region sets the climate that sets the heat and colour. The North China Plain (Henan, Hebei,
      Shandong) grows cluster-type facing-heaven chillies; the Northwest (Xinjiang, Gansu) is the
      colour-pepper belt; the high-heat belt (Hunan, Guizhou, Sichuan, Chongqing) grows the hot
      varieties; local speciality regions (Shaanxi, Yunnan) add distinctive types like Shaanxi 8819.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why variety is the first decision</h2>
    <p>
      Heat, colour and flavour are set by the variety and the ripening climate — and they vary
      independently. Naming the variety (or a precise SHU window) is what turns "chilli powder" into a
      reproducible product. For the full variety-to-SHU mapping, see our
      <a href="/heat-levels/medium-heat/">heat level pages</a> or the
      <a href="https://docs.drychillis.com/chilli-varieties/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">full variety guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How to choose a variety</h2>
    <p>
      Start from the application, then pick the variety that fits the heat band — not the other way
      around. A chilli for hot sauce needs the high-heat or extra-hot band; a chilli for colouring
      needs a sweet or low-heat variety with high ASTA. The three questions, in order: <strong>what
      heat (SHU)</strong>, <strong>what colour (ASTA)</strong>, and <strong>what form</strong> (whole,
      flakes, powder, segments). Answer those and the variety shortlist is usually two or three names.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a worked example, see our <a href="/blog/how-to-specify-chilli-powder/">chilli powder
      specification guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Region drives character, not just heat</h2>
    <p>
      Two varieties at the same SHU can still differ in colour, aroma and oil content, because those
      traits are set by <em>where</em> the chilli ripens as much as by the variety itself. The
      Northwest colour-pepper belt (Xinjiang, Gansu) produces high-colour, low-heat peppers because of
      long sunny days and low humidity; the high-heat belt (Hunan, Guizhou, Sichuan) produces hotter,
      more aromatic peppers in its humid climate. Naming both the variety <em>and</em> the growing
      region in a specification is what locks a flavour profile down batch after batch.
    </p>

